find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,678,279,24438,3793,304,279,14461,315,198,59,9697,59,26888,90,17,92,488,1124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,1477,279,2629,315,678,279,24438,3793,304,279,14461,315,1124,1188,59,26888,90,17,92,488,1124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,11035,701,582,686,990,279,9544,20855,57817,13,576,9544,20855,57817,5302,429,1447,59,9640,11520,26888,90,17,92,488,1124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,92,284,1124,1242,15159,74,28,15,92,47822,16,17,92,1124,6863,316,90,16,17,15170,74,92,19788,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,92,19788,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,198,59,2533,1654,1184,311,8253,892,3793,304,419,14461,525,24438,13,362,4647,374,24438,421,2176,1124,11520,26888,90,17,11035,8,323,1124,11520,26888,58,18,15370,18,11035,8,525,9226,311,7546,13430,429,1102,304,24438,5109,13,44763,11,1124,1188,59,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,11035,8,374,24438,421,17767,16,17,12646,57758,374,1496,11,323,1124,1188,59,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,57758,374,24438,421,17767,74,57758,374,264,5248,315,220,18,13,15277,11,17767,74,57758,1969,26553,2176,4682,25,17767,74,57758,1969,387,264,5248,315,220,18,323,17767,16,17,12646,57758,1969,387,1496,382,10061,594,1140,279,3204,2750,315,17767,74,59,7731],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"bad2ba23cee026da0a99470aa297f397d6e8d2cf24b7204f5a9bccf3fda17178"},"seconds":26.96138834953308,"seed":695817121362541700,"stop":"eos","text":"- \\(k = 0\\): \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even)\n- \\(k = 3\\): \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd)\n- \\(k = 6\\): \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even)\n- \\(k = 9\\): \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd)\n- \\(k = 12\\): \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even)\n\nSo, the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions are \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). We will now calculate the terms for these values of \\(k\\):\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 66528\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n\\]\n\nAdding these rational terms together, we get:\n\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n- \\(k = 0\\): \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even)\n- \\(k = 3\\): \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd)\n- \\(k = 6\\): \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even)\n- \\(k = 9\\): \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd)\n- \\(k = 12\\): \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even)\n\nSo, the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions are \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). We will now calculate the terms for these values of \\(k\\):\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 66528\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81\n\\]\n\nAdding these rational terms together, we get:\n\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:445"}
